      <h1>C-Rate Testing</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> C-rate testing runs a battery at <strong>multiples of its rated
        capacity</strong> — 1C drains a 100 Ah battery at 100 A, 2C at 200 A — to verify <strong>power capability,
        fast-charge performance and rate capacity</strong>. The higher the C-rate the battery sustains within its
        limits, the more demanding the application it can serve.
      </div>

      <h2>What the rate means</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>C-rate</th><th>For a 100 Ah cell</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>1C</strong></td><td>100 A</td><td>~1 hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>2C</strong></td><td>200 A</td><td>~30 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>3C</strong></td><td>300 A</td><td>~20 min</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">A 0.5C charge takes ~2 hours; the C-rate scales current inversely with time.</p>

      <h2>Why it matters</h2>
      <p>
        The C-rate is the bridge between the datasheet and the application: an EV cell must sustain multi-C
        discharge for acceleration and multi-C <em>charge</em> for fast charging, while a storage cell may never
        exceed 0.5C. The test verifies what the chemistry can actually deliver — including the heat it generates
        at high rates (see <a href="/temperature-testing/">temperature testing</a> and
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lithium/c-rate/">C-rate fundamentals</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>Interpretation</h2>
      <p>
        The same battery delivers <strong>less capacity at higher rates</strong> — a cell rated 100 Ah at 0.5C may
        give ~95 Ah at 2C, with the gap widening as the rate rises. The rate-capacity relationship is part of the
        specification, not a defect: it reflects the internal losses that grow with current
        (see <a href="/capacity-rates/">C20/C10/C5</a> for the lead-acid version of the same physics).
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> The C-rate is where a battery's application claim is verified — the datasheet
        promises capacity, but only the rate test proves what the battery can actually deliver when the job
        demands it fast.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Every demanding application is a rate application. The buyer who checks
        sustained C-rate — and the capacity at that rate — is verifying the real product, not the gentle-rate
        headline number.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

